---
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Can I visualize multiple HEX codes at once?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Paste a list of HEX codes into the color input and the preview updates immediately. You can use spaces, commas, new lines, semicolons, pipes, or a custom separator.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the visualizer support RGB, HSL, RGBA, and OKLCH?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Auto-detect accepts HEX, RGB, RGBA, HSL, and OKLCH values, including mixed-format lists. Dedicated format controls are also available when you want stricter input.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I compare more than one color palette?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Multi Group mode lets you create and compare up to four independent palettes. Each group can be edited separately while its preview remains visible.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are my palettes uploaded or stored on a server?",
    answer:
      "The tool runs in your browser and saves the current workspace in local browser storage. Sharing is performed through a generated URL or an exported workspace file.",
  },
];
---

<section class="seo-content" aria-labelledby="about-visualizer">
  <div class="seo-intro">
    <h2 id="about-visualizer">Visualize, compare, and refine colors in one workspace</h2>
    <p>
      Multi Hex Visualizer is a free browser-based workspace for turning raw color
      values into useful, readable palettes. Paste a handful of brand colors or a
      large set of tokens and see every swatch immediately. Unlike a basic hex
      color viewer, this multi hex tool keeps input, editing, comparison, and
      export together. There is no account to create and no file to upload before
      you can begin.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool is designed for web developers, product designers, illustrators,
      theme creators, and anyone who needs to visualize multiple hex codes without
      assembling a temporary design file. It also works as an RGB visualizer, HSL
      color visualizer, RGBA color visualizer, and OKLCH color visualizer. Use one
      format throughout a palette or let auto-detect read a mixed list.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="seo-columns">
    <article>
      <h3>Paste multiple colors, not one at a time</h3>
      <p>
        A conventional color picker is useful when you already know which color
        you want. Multi Hex Visualizer is built for the opposite problem: you
        already have many values and need to understand them together. Paste
        multiple HEX colors separated by commas, spaces, new lines, semicolons,
        pipes, or your own delimiter. The parser keeps commas and spaces inside
        RGB, RGBA, HSL, and OKLCH functions intact, so realistic CSS color lists
        remain easy to import.
      </p>
      <p>
        The live count and validation message make large lists easier to check.
        Valid colors remain available even when another entry needs attention.
        You can work with up to roughly 250 visible colors, reorder them, assign
        custom names, and use undo or redo while refining the set. If you searched
        for a way to “add multiple hex numbers,” this is the color-focused workflow
        you are looking for: paste the codes once and inspect them as a complete
        visual system.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3>Choose the view that reveals the right relationship</h3>
      <p>
        A color grid is excellent for scanning individual swatches, while stripes
        expose the boundaries between neighboring colors. The visualizer includes
        sharp grids, rounded tiles, circular swatches, vertical and horizontal
        stripes, diagonal bands, a radial view, and a typography preview. Switch
        layouts without rebuilding the palette, then enter fullscreen when you
        want the colors to fill the display.
      </p>
      <p>
        Interactive mode adds inspection and editing. Select a swatch to adjust
        its hue, saturation, value, name, or exact color value. Visualize Only
        removes labels and controls from the artwork, creating a clean multi color
        visualizer for review or presentation. Because the same data powers every
        view, you can explore composition without introducing copy-and-paste
        errors.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <section class="comparison" aria-labelledby="compare-palettes">
    <div>
      <h2 id="compare-palettes">Compare HEX colors and complete palettes</h2>
      <p>
        Single Group mode is a focused hex palette visualizer for one collection.
        Multi Group mode turns the workspace into a color palette comparison tool
        with as many as four independent groups. Use it to compare light and dark
        themes, evaluate brand directions, review design-token revisions, or place
        several client palettes side by side. Group tabs keep editing predictable,
        and the combined preview makes differences visible without merging the
        source lists.
      </p>
      <p>
        This makes the app useful as a multiple HEX color comparison tool as well
        as a multi RGB visualizer. A developer can compare legacy RGB values with
        new OKLCH tokens; a designer can check whether two palettes share too many
        similar tones; and a theme author can inspect how accent colors balance
        across separate variants. Sorting by hue, brightness, or saturation can
        reveal patterns that are difficult to see in an unsorted text file.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside aria-label="Supported color workflows">
      <h3>Built for real palette work</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Paste and auto-detect mixed color formats</li>
        <li>Compare up to four independent color groups</li>
        <li>Edit, name, sort, reverse, and reorder swatches</li>
        <li>Copy values or export PNG, SVG, CSS, JSON, and TXT</li>
        <li>Share a restorable workspace without creating an account</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <div class="seo-columns">
    <article>
      <h3>Move between HEX, RGB, HSL, and modern color spaces</h3>
      <p>
        Color work rarely stays in one notation. A design handoff may contain HEX
        codes, a stylesheet may use RGB or RGBA, and a modern token system may use
        OKLCH. This multiple RGB color visualizer accepts all of them and provides
        conversions for the selected swatch. You can copy the active palette in a
        consistent output format or preserve the original mixed formats when that
        better matches your source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alpha-aware RGBA values are supported, and HSL or OKLCH inputs can be
        inspected alongside their visual result. That makes the workspace useful
        for learning a color format as well as production work. Instead of
        translating values in one tab and checking them in another, you can
        visualize RGB values online, compare the result, and export the finished
        palette from the same screen.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3>Export a palette you can actually use</h3>
      <p>
        A useful color swatch visualizer should not trap the result. Copy All
        produces a clean list using your chosen format and separator. Data exports
        include CSS, JSON, and plain text for development workflows. PNG and SVG
        exports capture the current visualization for mood boards, reviews, and
        documentation. A shareable link restores the palette and relevant view
        settings; very large workspaces can be saved as a file instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your current workspace is stored locally in the browser so an accidental
        refresh does not erase it. The project is open source, making it suitable
        for teams that prefer transparent tools. Whether you call it a bulk HEX
        color visualizer, hex color grid generator, or palette viewer, the goal is
        the same: spend less time moving codes between utilities and more time
        judging the colors themselves.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <h2 id="faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    {faqs.map((faq) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{faq.question}</summary>
        <p>{faq.answer}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="contact" id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
    <div>
      <h2 id="contact-title">Contact and project feedback</h2>
      <p>
        Found a bug, have an accessibility suggestion, or want to request a color
        workflow? Open an issue on GitHub so the report stays visible and can be
        tracked with the project.
      </p>
    </div>
    <a href="https://github.com/mshareef-git/Multi-Hex-Visualizer/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      Open a GitHub issue
    </a>
  </section>
</section>

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: faq.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: faq.answer },
  })),
})} />
